#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536017} :activity/id #uuid "259db048-1a55-58f9-8b73-5acef3edbeaf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/id #uuid "2d9b53c7-d4e4-5c8d-ad01-c539428002fb" :topic/math-academy-id 1401} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ae1d17-586d-5656-9819-82e44f1f99e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420278} {:answer/id #uuid "3f50700d-4949-56a2-a27b-18e517742a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420282} {:answer/id #uuid "6627b401-f702-5235-8c84-b1a54bb3416f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420287} {:answer/id #uuid "672c97f7-89dc-5157-8a28-9704883db291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420288} {:answer/id #uuid "f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420296} :answer-field/id #uuid "3706a6bf-e668-594b-b09d-444f45050912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9ddb293-a141-5410-8588-0be8dbbb83c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126928" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "3ca2cc69-4942-5fb5-9b30-e5ad1d2434e4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70efccd7-a4ec-51be-bc8c-eb43d5f222fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387756} {:answer/id #uuid "96372565-ba69-5c28-8a4c-9b6fe589c9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387757} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae2f33e-2d17-55f9-a4c0-3b66e6a17f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4x \\\\ 4y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387758} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0546af-1dba-55cb-8fd2-f11d650496c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387759} {:answer/id #uuid "7b433770-bc6f-5296-8bf1-d6c00fb0124d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387756} :answer-field/id #uuid "95039db2-a6d4-5d92-b575-f7c5188c2109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "568e518b-6dee-5582-961d-6548648b3418" :question/math-academy-id "q-126918" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=4,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3dccba39-3b8d-5505-98cb-d31f375f766d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68a410-e43c-5b13-be15-d2995454689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273172} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6e705b-41df-5a7e-a7c8-b32bd10ff563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273599} {:answer/id #uuid "b73f0c31-46cc-5227-a27f-02cfff35bbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273977} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a9ef27-590b-5785-90f3-ec1a5aee993d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274240} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bf1cd5-6bd3-564e-ae75-e3df834ef08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273599} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f06a1b-3c11-5162-98f1-6600333a0c5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22545d4e-216d-56de-bbb1-cb8300d938d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-149683" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=6,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e4c0550-e134-561a-97d0-d8c0a3c73e85"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bde73f4-acbe-5a30-bd8d-32583815fc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445272} {:answer/id #uuid "8a98e20c-8619-5648-a5cf-3ffe0e3b9b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445276} {:answer/id #uuid "da1537f7-60f2-5487-98fb-d8eb37445eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445282} {:answer/id #uuid "e881b58b-f16e-577e-bb7a-de715dbd1a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{co